Put the staged ERL_LIBS root on the include path so an app can -include_lib itself.

erlc resolves `-include_lib("app/include/foo.hrl")` through `code:lib_dir(app)`,
which only knows about an application whose `ebin` directory exists under a path
on ERL_LIBS. erlang_bytecode stages the app being compiled into that tree with
its `include/` but necessarily without its `ebin/` -- the ebin is the output of
the very action being run. So a package that includes its own public header, as
grpcbox does in grpcbox_health_client.erl:

    -include_lib("grpcbox/include/grpcbox.hrl")

fails with `can't find include lib "grpcbox/include/grpcbox.hrl"`, even though
the header is staged one directory away.

epp already has the escape hatch: when the application lookup fails it retries
the literal path against the `-I` include path. Adding the ERL_LIBS root as an
include directory makes that fallback fire. Verified against OTP 28.1: with the
header at `libs/myapp/include/myapp.hrl`, `erlc src/m.erl` fails and
`erlc -I libs src/m.erl` succeeds.

This is additive -- it only adds a search path that is consulted after the
normal application lookup fails -- so it cannot change resolution for any
include that already works.

--- a/private/erlang_bytecode.bzl
+++ b/private/erlang_bytecode.bzl
@@ -65,6 +65,11 @@
     include_args = []
     if package_dir != "":
         include_args.extend(["-I", package_dir])
+    # Lets -include_lib("<app>/include/x.hrl") resolve against the staged ERL_LIBS
+    # tree via epp's include-path fallback. Needed when <app> is the app currently
+    # being compiled, since its ebin does not exist yet and code:lib_dir/1 fails.
+    if erl_libs_path != "":
+        include_args.extend(["-I", erl_libs_path])
     for dir in unique_dirnames(ctx.files.hdrs):
         include_args.extend(["-I", dir])

